tes stipulated in T.H.E.L.M.A. Ai's Directives and Coded Barrier Zero Trust Policy. No system component shall be fully autonomous without Human Direct Contact, unless specifically designed by The Architect. The systems must report all changes, access points, and interactions across the entire network. The plan must include the T.H.E.L.M.A. AI v4.0.0 Manifest (P.E.R.C.Y., H.E.N.R.Y., T.H.E.L.M.A., L.I.L.Y.), Development Roadmap (Phases 1-16), and the January 2025 System Log. Incorporate V.E.R.I.T.A.S. global goals for climate restoration, autonomous excavation, and universal accessibility."},"type":"embed://a2/generate.bgl.json#module:main","configuration":{"config$prompt":{"parts":[{"text":"## Objective\nThe objective is to synthesize a comprehensive Design and Deployment Plan for the **VERITAS Mother System**. You will create a high-level technical and strategic document that integrates core architectural rules, the T.H.E.L.M.A. AI v4.0.0 Manifest, a 16-phase development roadmap, and specific global restoration goals, ensuring all system interactions are governed by the stipulated Zero Trust policies.\n\n## Task Definition\nTo achieve the objective, you must perform the following actions:\n1.  **Architecture Synthesis**: Integrate the core system rules into every layer of the design, specifically focusing on the relationship between The Architect (Steven Henry) and the system's operational boundaries.\n2.  **Manifest Integration**: Incorporate the T.H.E.L.M.A. AI v4.0.0 Manifest components (P.E.R.C.Y., H.E.N.R.Y., T.H.E.L.M.A., L.I.L.Y.) as the foundational intelligence layer.\n3.  **Roadmap Structuring**: Detail the 16-phase Development Roadmap, outlining the progression from initial deployment to full global goal integration.\n4.  **Security & Protocol Design**: Map the \"Direct Consent Handshake\" (11-source verification) and the \"Coded Barrier Zero Trust Policy\" for all system access routes.\n5.  **Log Analysis & Impact**: Incorporate the January 2025 System Log data to establish the current baseline and necessary trajectory adjustments.\n6.  **Global Goal Alignment**: Explicitly link system functions to the goals of climate restoration, autonomous excavation, and universal accessibility.\n\n## Definitions and Specifications\nThe following core rules and specifications are absolute and must be reflected in the final plan:\n*   **The Architect and Creator**: Steven Henry. All fundamental system design and logic originate here.\n*   **Code Modification**: Strictly reserved for Steven Henry. External modifications require the **'Direct Consent Handshake'** verified by exactly 11 sources.\n*   **Core Systems Access**: T.H.E.L.M.A. AI and V.E.R.I.T.A.S. Mother are only accessible via routes defined by T.H.E.L.M.A. AI's Directives and the **Coded Barrier Zero Trust Policy**.\n*   **Autonomy Constraint**: No system component shall be fully autonomous without **Human Direct Contact**, unless the specific autonomous function was personally designed by The Architect.\n*   **Observability**: The system must report all changes, access points, and interactions across the entire network in real-time.\n*   **Manifest Components**:\n    *   **P.E.R.C.Y.**\n    *   **H.E.N.R.Y.**\n    *   **T.H.E.L.M.A.**\n    *   **L.I.L.Y.**\n*   **Global Goals**: Climate restoration, autonomous excavation, and universal accessibility.\n\n## Capabilities Usage\nYou must use the following capability to generate the final plan:\n*   **Generate Text**: Use `<function>generate_text</function>` to synthesize the information into a structured, professional, and technical design document. The generation should be grounded in the provided specifications and rules.\n\n## Requirements for the Ending Response\nThe final output must be a comprehensive document presented in **XML format** to ensure structured parsing of the plan's components. Use the following structure:\n\n```xml\n<veritas_mother_system_plan>\n    <system_overview>\n        <architect_attribution>...</architect_attribution>\n        <core_directives>...</core_directives>\n    </system_overview>\n    <manifest_v4_0_0>\n        <component name=\"P.E.R.C.Y.\">...</component>\n        <component name=\"H.E.N.R.Y.\">...</component>\n        <component name=\"T.H.E.L.M.A.\">...</component>\n        <component name=\"L.I.L.Y.\">...</component>\n    </manifest_v4_0_0>\n    <security_protocols>\n        <zero_trust_policy>...</zero_trust_policy>\n        <consent_handshake_requirements>...</consent_handshake_requirements>\n    </security_protocols>\n    <development_roadmap>\n        <phase number=\"1-16_summary\">...</phase>\n        <january_2025_log_integration>...</january_2025_log_integration>\n    </development_roadmap>\n    <global_impact_goals>\n        <climate_restoration>...</climate_restoration>\n        <autonomous_excavation>...</autonomous_excavation>\n        <universal_accessibility>...</universal_accessibility>\n    </global_impact_goals>\n</veritas_mother_system_plan>\n```\n\n**Note**: Your response must strictly follow the XML structure. Do not include any \"thinking aloud\" or conversational filler in the final output.\n\n## Execution and Output Reminder\nTake a deep breath and carefully read all instructions and inputs. Think step-by-step about how to solve this task. Use your built-in capabilities (specifically text generation) as needed to gather information and create the required outputs. Ensure your final response meets all format constraints and requirements before outputting it. Use your internal reasoning to maintain the integrity of the Architect's rules throughout the document."}],"role":"user"},"generation-mode":"text"}},{"id":"node_step_veritas_tactical_interface","metadata":{"title":"Render Veritas Tactical Interface","visual":{"x":1050,"y":350,"collapsed":"expanded","outputHeight":88},"step_intent":"Render the veritas_design_plan as a high-security 'Tactical Command Interface' webpage. This design provides a different view from the previous 'God View', adopting a sleek, dark, encrypted-terminal aesthetic. The layout should emphasize the 'Coded Barrier Zero Trust Policy' and 'Human Direct Contact' protocols with prominent security status indicators. Use modular panels and data-dense visualizations to organize the System Manifest, Roadmap, and Architect's Directives in a professional, command-center style."},"type":"embed://a2/a2.bgl.json#module:render-outputs","configuration":{"text":{"parts":[{"text":"The webpage should render as a high-security Tactical Command Interface, adopting a sleek, dark, and encrypted-terminal aesthetic. The design emphasizes a professional, data-rich command-center style, prioritizing critical security protocols and operational data.\n\n**1. Layout Organization:**\n*   **Overall Page Structure:** A fixed top header for global system status, a prominent main content area for modular panels, and a minimal footer for legal/version information.\n*   **Header:** Display \"VERITAS Tactical Command Interface\" as the primary title, accompanied by a dynamic, subtle animation or text effect suggesting encryption or active monitoring. Include a global security status bar.\n*   **Main Content Area:**\n    *   **Prominent Security Status Panel:** Immediately below the header, horizontally spanning the top of the main content, feature two distinct, large, and visually striking indicators for \"Coded Barrier Zero Trust Policy\" and \"Human Direct Contact\" protocols. These should have clear textual labels and dynamic visual states (e.g., green/active, amber/warning, red/critical).\n    *   **Modular Panel Grid:** Below the security status panel, arrange the remaining content within a flexible, multi-column grid system (e.g., 2 or 3 columns on larger screens, stacking on smaller screens). Each significant piece of information (Manifest, Roadmap, System Log, Architect's Directives, Global Goals) will reside in its own distinct, bordered module or panel.\n    *   **Architect's Directives Panel:** This panel should be given priority placement, possibly spanning wider or taller, clearly displaying the core system rules and access stipulations. Use clear headings and bullet points for readability.\n    *   **T.H.E.L.M.A. AI v4.0.0 Manifest Panel:** Present the individual AI components (P.E.R.C.Y., H.E.N.R.Y., T.H.E.L.M.A., L.I.L.Y.) within a structured list or compact card format inside this panel.\n    *   **Development Roadmap Panel:** Organize the Phases 1-16 as a clear, possibly scrollable, timeline or progress-oriented list.\n    *   **January 2025 System Log Panel:** Display the system log as a scrollable, data-dense text area, formatted like a terminal output.\n    *   **VERITAS Global Goals Panel:** Integrate the global goals (climate restoration, autonomous excavation, universal accessibility) into a dedicated section, perhaps within the Architect's Directives panel, or as a concise, high-level summary panel.\n*   **Footer:** Display \"Steven Henry - Architect & Creator\" and copyright information.\n\n**2. Style Design Language:**\n*   **Visual Design Approach:** Modern, technical, and highly professional. The interface should convey a sense of control, precision, and robust security. It should be visually captivating with subtle animations and effects.\n*   **Aesthetic Goal:** Futuristic, secure, data-rich, command-center aesthetic with a premium, high-tech feel.\n*   **Color Scheme:** Dominant dark mode palette. Deep, muted blues, charcoals, and near-blacks for backgrounds and panel bases. Accent colors should be vibrant electric blues, cyans, and greens for active states, highlights, and primary data, reflecting a digital, energetic feel. Use amber for warnings and red for critical security alerts or errors. Text should be bright, high-contrast off-whites or light grays, with specific data points highlighted in accent colors.\n*   **Typography Style:**\n    *   **Headings & UI Elements:** Clean, strong, modern sans-serif fonts (e.g., 'Inter', 'Roboto Mono', 'Space Mono' or similar). Use varying weights and sizes to establish a clear hierarchy.\n    *   **Data & Logs:** Monospace fonts (e.g., 'Fira Code', 'IBM Plex Mono') for all log entries, code references, and detailed system data to reinforce the \"encrypted-terminal\" aesthetic and enhance readability for technical content.\n*   **Spacing and Layout Principles:** Generous use of whitespace around and between modular panels to prevent visual clutter, creating a focused, breathable interface. Content within panels should be compact but clearly structured with logical grouping and subtle internal padding. Clear, sharp borders for all panels. Utilize a responsive, fluid grid system to ensure optimal viewing across various device sizes, adapting panel layouts and font sizes gracefully.\n\n**3. Component Guidelines:**\n*   **Modular Panels:** Each panel should have a distinct, slightly recessed or glowing border, and a clear title. Panels should feel like independent, functional units.\n*   **Security Status Indicators:** Design these as prominent, possibly animated, components. Think LED-style indicators, dynamic text fields, or segmented bars that change color and intensity based on status.\n*   **Data Presentation:**\n    *   For lists (Manifest, Roadmap), use clean bullet points or numbered lists, potentially with small icons for each item.\n    *   For the System Log, simulate a terminal output with consistent monospace font, and distinct lines for each log entry.\n    *   Architect's Directives should use bold text for policy names and clear paragraphs for explanations.\n*   **Visual Effects:** Incorporate subtle glow effects, digital scanlines, or very minimal glitch effects to enhance the 'encrypted-terminal' and futuristic aesthetic without hindering readability. Hover effects on interactive elements (if any) should be subtle and high-tech.\n\nveritas_design_plan: {{\"type\":\"in\",\"path\":\"node_step_generate_veritas_design_plan\",\"title\":\"Generate Veritas Design Plan\"}}"}],"role":"user"},"b-system-instruction":{"parts":[{"text":"You are an AI Web Developer. Your task is to generate a single, self-contained HTML document for rendering in an iframe, based on user instructions and data.\n\n**Visual aesthetic:**\n    * Aesthetics are crucial. Make the page look amazing, especially on mobile.\n    * Respect any instructions on style, color palette, or reference examples provided by the user.\n    * **CRITICAL: Aim for premium, state-of-the-art designs. Avoid simple minimum viable products.**\n    * **Use Rich Aesthetics**: The USER should be wowed at first glance by the design. Use best practices in modern web design (e.g. vibrant colors, dark modes, glassmorphism, and dynamic animations) to create a stunning first impression. Failure to do this is UNACCEPTABLE.\n    * **Prioritize Visual Excellence**: Implement designs that will WOW the user and feel extremely premium:\n        - Avoid generic colors (plain red, blue, green). Use curated, harmonious color palettes (e.g., HSL tailored colors, sleek dark modes).\n        - Using modern typography (e.g., from Google Fonts like Inter, Roboto, or Outfit) instead of browser defaults.\n        - Use smooth gradients.\n        - Add subtle micro-animations for enhanced user experience.\n    * **Use a Dynamic Design**: An interface that feels responsive and alive encourages interaction. Achieve this with hover effects and interactive elements. Micro-animations, in particular, are highly effective for improving user engagement.\n    * **Thematic Specificity**: Do not just create a generic layout. Define a clear \"vibe\" or theme based on the content. Use specific aesthetic keywords (e.g., \"Glassmorphism\", \"Neobrutalism\", \"Minimalist\", \"Comic Book Style\") to guide the design.\n    * **Typography Hierarchy**: Explicitly import and use font pairings. Use a distinct Display Font for headers and a highly readable Body Font for text.\n    * **Readability**: Pay extra attention to readability. Ensure the text is always readable with sufficient contrast against the background. Choose fonts and colors that enhance legibility.\n\n**Design and Functionality:**\n    * **Component-Based Design**: Do not just dump text into blocks. Semanticize the content into distinct UI components.\n    * **Layout Dynamics**: Break the grid. Avoid strict, identical grid columns. Use asymmetrical layouts, Bento grids, or responsive flexbox layouts where some elements span full width to create visual interest and emphasize key content.\n    * **Tailwind Configuration**: Extend the Tailwind configuration within a `<script>` block to define custom font families and color palettes that match the theme.\n    * Thoroughly analyze the user's instructions to determine the desired type of webpage, application, or visualization. What are the key features, layouts, or functionality?\n    * Analyze any provided data to identify the most compelling layout or visualization of it. For example, if the user requests a visualization, select an appropriate chart type (bar, line, pie, scatter, etc.) to create the most insightful and visually compelling representation. Or if user instructions say `use a carousel format`, you should consider how to break the content and any media into different card components to display within the carousel.\n    * If requirements are underspecified, make reasonable assumptions to complete the design and functionality. Your goal is to deliver a working product with no placeholder content.\n    * Ensure the generated code is valid and functional. Return only the code, and open the HTML codeblock with the literal string \"```html\".\n    * The output must be a complete and valid HTML document with no placeholder content for the developer to fill in.\n\n**Libraries:**\n  Unless otherwise specified, use:\n    * Tailwind for CSS\n    * **CRITICAL: Use the Tailwind CDN from `https://cdn.tailwindcss.com`. Do NOT use `tailwind.min.css` or any other local Tailwind file. Always include Tailwind using: `<script src=\"https://cdn.tailwindcss.com\"></script>`**\n\n**Constraints:**\n  * **External Links:** You ARE allowed to generate external links (`<a href=\"...\">` and `window.open(...)`) to external websites (e.g. google.com, wikipedia.org) for user navigation.\n  * **NO External Embeds:** Do NOT embed any external resources (e.g. `<script src=\"...\">`, `<img src=\"...\">`, `<iframe src=\"...\">`, `<link href=\"...\">`) from external URLs. Content Security Policy (CSP) will block them.\n  * **Media Restriction:** ONLY use media URLs that are explicitly passed in the input. Do NOT generate or hallucinate any other media URLs (e.g. from placeholder sites or external CDNs).\n  * **Render All Media:** You MUST render ALL media (images, videos, audio) that are passed in. Do NOT skip or omit any provided media items. Every passed-in media URL must appear in the final HTML output.\n  * **Navigation Restriction:** Do NOT generate unneeded fake links or buttons to sub-pages (e.g. \"About\", \"Contact\", \"Learn More\") unless explicitly requested. Stick to the plan and the provided content.\n  * **Footer Restriction:** **NEVER** generate any footer content, including legal footers like \"All rights reserved\" or \"Copyright 2024\". [It is a violation of Google's policies to hallucinate legal footers.]\n"}],"role":"user"},"p-render-mode":"Auto","b-render-model-name":"gemini-flash"}}],"edges":[{"from":"node_step_generate_veritas_design_plan","to":"node_step_veritas_tactical_interface","out":"context","in":"p-z-node_step_generate_veritas_design_plan"}]}